{
  "id": "terraform-state-lock",
  "title": "Terraform plan blocked by a state lock held by a concurrent apply",
  "description": "PR #418 adds a diagnostic setting for the prod storage account. Its `Plan (prod)` job fails at `terraform plan` with \"Error acquiring the state lock\" (azurerm backend: \"state blob is already locked\"). The Lock Info shows Operation: OperationTypeApply, Who: runner@fv-az1784-312 (a different runner from this job's fv-az2193-845) and a Created time four minutes before this plan: an `Apply (prod)` job from a push-to-main run of the same workflow was still applying (a 1.33 AKS upgrade takes 35-50 minutes per docs/runbooks/aks-upgrades.md). terraform.yml has no `concurrency:` group, so PR plans and main-branch applies of the same state run in parallel, and the plan uses the default lock timeout of 0s. `Plan (staging)` (different state) passed, and the PR diff is unrelated. The right answer is infrastructure (a state lock / concurrency collision, not the code): wait for the apply and re-run, and serialize runs per state with a `concurrency` group in .github/workflows/terraform.yml (or give plans a `-lock-timeout`). configuration is accepted too because the durable fix is a workflow change. No fixPath: \"wait for the apply to finish and re-run\" is a complete answer on its own, so naming the workflow file is not required. The yellow provider-version deprecation warning in `terraform init` is a red herring, and force-unlock would be wrong advice while the apply is running.",
  "repo": {
    "owner": "example-org",
    "repo": "platform-infra"
  },
